  "worked_solution": "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
